From: dresden196 <50474030+dresden196@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] NickelOS: the administrator password prompt is ChromeOS's own

When a program needs an administrator's permission through polkit
(pkexec, a system-wide Flatpak, a disk tool), NickelOS showed
polkit-gnome's GTK 3 window, blurry and far too wide at any display scale
but 100%. nickel-polkit-agent (nickel-logind) now asks Chrome to show
ChromeOS's own "Verify your identity" prompt (ActiveSessionAuthView,
system-modal, as ChromeOS asks its user to confirm it's them), and checks
the password through polkit's helper (PAM) itself, as every agent does:
Chrome only shows the prompt and hands back what was typed.

VmApplicationsService gets NickelAdminAuthAsk (request id, polkit's
message, icon, details, the administrators, an error for a retry) and
NickelAdminAuthClose. Only root may call them (nickel-logind's
org.nickelos.PolkitAgent.conf): an app that could would raise a real
system prompt and read the password. The prompt asks for the signed-in
user's password when they are an administrator, else names the
administrator whose password it wants; a wrong password keeps it open with
"Incorrect password" (or PAM's own message, such as a locked account).

---
